   },
   "source": [
    "# Machine Learning: Introduction\n",
    "\n",
    "## Elsa Ferreira Gomes\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2025/2026\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {
    "Digscope.jpg": {
     "image/jpeg": ""
    }
   },
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "# Machine Learning \n",
    "\n",
    "### Problem examples\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Classification using audio data: heart sounds and other signals\n",
    "\n",
    "![Digscope.jpg](attachment:Digscope.jpg)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {
    "Anuran.jpg": {
     "image/jpeg": ""
    }
   },
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "# Machine Learning \n",
    "## Problem examples\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Classification anuran sounds\n",
    "\n",
    "![Anuran.jpg](attachment:Anuran.jpg)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "# Machine Learning \n",
    "### Problem examples\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Flower classification\n",
    "Classify types of Iris flowers (setosa, versicolor, or virginica). How would you do it?\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ML-course/master/master/notebooks/images/01_iris.jpeg\" alt=\"ml\" style=\"width: 75%;\"/>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "# Machine Learning \n",
    "## Problem examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Spotify Popularity Prediction\n",
    "- Predict House Prices\n",
    "- Natural Language Processing with Disaster Tweets\n",
    "- Email spam detection system\n",
    "- Histopathologic Oral Cancer Detection\n",
    "- Identify numbers in a handwritten zip code\n",
    "- Face recognition\n",
    "- .....\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "# Machine Learning \n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\"A computer program is said to learn from **experience E** with respect to some class of **tasks T** and **performance measure P** if its performance at tasks in *T*, as measured by *P*, improves with experience *E*.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "Tom Mitchell"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Example: A spam filter learns if, after seeing more labeled emails (experience E), it gets better at classifying new emails (task T) according to its accuracy (performance measure P).\n",
    " - Task (T): Classifying emails as spam or not spam.   \n",
    " - Performance Measure (P): The percentage of emails correctly classified\n",
    " - Experience (E): A dataset of labeled emails (spam / not spam)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {
    "ML-Types-1.png": {
     "image/png": ""
    }
   },
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "# Machine Learning \n",
    "\n",
    "![ML-Types-1.png](attachment:ML-Types-1.png)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "## Types of machine learning\n",
    "- **Supervised Learning**: learn a _model_ $f$ from _labeled data_ $(X,y)$ (ground truth)\n",
    "    - Given a new input _X_, predict the right output _y_\n",
    "    - Given examples of stars and galaxies, identify new objects in the sky\n",
    "- **Unsupervised Learning**: explore the structure of the data (X) to extract meaningful information\n",
    "    - Given inputs _X_, find which ones are special, similar, anomalous, ...\n",
    "- **Reinforcement Learning**: develop an agent that improves its performance based on interactions with the environment \n",
    "\n",
    "Note: Practical ML systems can combine many types in one system."
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {
    "Classif_vs_Regr.png": {
     "image/png": ""
    }
   },
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "## Supervised Learning Problem\n",
    " - Outcome measurement $Y$ (also called dependent variable, response, target).\n",
    " - Vector of $p$ predictor measurements $X$ (or inputs, regressors, covariates, features, independent variables).\n",
    " - In the **regression problem**, $Y$ is quantitative (e.g price, blood pressure).\n",
    " - In the **classification problem**, $Y$ takes values in a finite, unordered set (e.g survived/died, digit 0-9, cancer class of tissue sample).\n",
    " - The training data ($x_1$, $y_1$), . . . ,($x_N$ , $y_N$) are observations (examples, instances) of these measurements.\n",
    "\n",
    "![Classif_vs_Regr.png](attachment:Classif_vs_Regr.png)\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "## Supervised Learning Problem\n",
    "### Objectives\n",
    "On the basis of the training data we would like to:\n",
    " - Accurately predict **unseen test cases**.\n",
    " - Understand which inputs (and how) affect the outcome.\n",
    " - Assess the quality of our predictions and inferences."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "## Supervised Learning Problem\n",
    "### The aim \n",
    "\n",
    "Our goal is to find a **useful approximation** $\\hat{f}(x)$  of  $f(x)$ \n",
    "\n",
    "- $f(x)$ is the function that generates the phenomenon it is **unknown**\n",
    "- What is the best **approximation**?\n",
    "- How do we measure the **goodness** of an approximation?\n",
    "\n",
    "- How do we **find the best approximation** of $f(x)$ (or at least a very good one)?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "<img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ML-course/master/master/notebooks/images/01_supervised.png\" alt=\"ml\" style=\"width:60%\"/>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "##### Representation: input features and labels\n",
    "\n",
    "- We can manually define a number of input features (variables), e.g. length and width of leaves\n",
    "- Every 'example' is a point in a (possibly high-dimensional) space\n",
    "    \n",
    "<img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ML-course/master/master/notebooks/images/01_terminology.png\" alt=\"ml\" style=\"float: left; width: 50%;\"/>\n",
    "<img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ML-course/master/master/notebooks/images/01_iris3d.png\" alt=\"ml\" style=\"float: left; width: 35%;\"/>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "#### Classification\n",
    "\n",
    "- Predict a _class label_ (category), discrete and unordered\n",
    "    - Can be _binary_ (e.g. spam/not spam) or _multi-class_ (e.g. letter recognition)\n",
    "    - Many classifiers can return a _confidence_ per class\n",
    "- The predictions of the model yield a _decision boundary_ separating the classes"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "#### Regression\n",
    "- Predict a continuous value, e.g. temperature\n",
    "    - Target variable is numeric\n",
    "    - Some algorithms can return a _confidence interval_\n",
    "- Find the relationship between predictors and the target."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "## Unsupervised Learning Problem\n",
    "- No outcome variable \n",
    "  - just a set of predictors (features) measured on a set of samples.\n",
    "- Explore the structure of the data to extract information\n",
    "- Different from supervised learning, \n",
    "  - but can be useful as a pre-processing step for supervised learning."
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {
    "data%20clusters.jpg": {
     "image/jpeg": ""
    }
   },
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "#### Clustering\n",
    "\n",
    "- Organize information into meaningful subgroups (clusters)\n",
    "- Objects in cluster share certain degree of similarity (and dissimilarity to other clusters)\n",
    "\n",
    "![data%20clusters.jpg](attachment:data%20clusters.jpg)\n",
    "    \n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "### Reinforcement learning\n",
    "\n",
    "- Develop an agent that improves its performance based on interactions with the environment\n",
    "- Search a space of actions and states\n",
    "- _Reward function_ defines how well the actions works\n",
    "- Learn a series of actions (policy) that maximizes reward through exploration\n",
    "\n",
    "- Example: \n",
    "  - Games like Chess, Go (AlphaGo/AlphaZero), Atari ...\n",
    "  - Autonomous Vehicles: Self-driving cars can use RL to optimize driving strategies in simulations,logistics (truck platooning) \n",
    "  - Robotics: manipulation, locomotion (like quadrupeds or humanoids learn to walk, run...), drone navigation (like learn obstacle avoidance)...\n",
    "     "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "<img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ML-course/master/master/notebooks/images/01_rl2.png\" alt=\"ml\" style=\"width: 50%;\"/>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {
    "car_price_regr.jfif": {
     "image/jpeg": ""
    }
   },
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "# Machine Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "## Example: Predicting Used Car Prices\n",
    "\n",
    "- We have observations (experience)\n",
    "- We assume there is a function that outputs the position given the (car) age\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "price = f(car.age)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "![car_price_regr.jfif](attachment:car_price_regr.jfif)\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "metadata": {
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
